talk(563,'1982-09-13','A. S. Schwarz','P001032-P001038','Gauge symmetry, topological strings and mirror particles',r"""The actual entry occupies P001033--P001036. A discussion of subjects to study leads to gauge covariantization, symmetry breaking and vacuum topology. Schwarz then describes strings whose holonomy reverses electric charge or exchanges ordinary and mirror matter. The printed group-theoretic criterion lacks a crucial stabilizer hypothesis. The final quantum-stability question and astrophysical anecdotes do not specify enough data for an independently identifiable theorem.""")
add(563,'Gauge covariantization and the connection transformation','P001033','construction',r"""For \(G\subset SO(n)\), replace \(\partial_\mu\) by \(\partial_\mu+A_\mu\), use \(F_{\mu\nu}=\partial_\mu A_\nu-\partial_\nu A_\mu+[A_\mu,A_\nu]\), and add a curvature-square term. The notes give \(\phi\mapsto g\phi\), \(A\mapsto gAg^{-1}+dg\,g^{-1}\).""",r"""For a connection \(D=d+A\) acting on a left representation and \(\phi^g=g\phi\), covariance \(D^g(g\phi)=gD\phi\) requires \(A^g=gAg^{-1}-dg\,g^{-1}\). Then \(F^g=gFg^{-1}\), and invariant inner products give gauge-invariant matter and Yang--Mills kinetic terms.""",r"""The local gauge principle is the content of \citet[pp.~191--195]{yangmills1954conservation}. The sign repair follows by expanding \(d(g\phi)\), independently of convention choices in that paper.""",('yangmills1954conservation',),literal='false as written',verification='read the result',reconstruction='The left action and sign of the covariant derivative are kept exactly as in the notebook; only the inconsistent connection sign is emended.')
add(563,'Perturbative renormalizability after gauging','P001034','claim',r"""The statement attributed to 't Hooft says that if \(L\) is renormalizable, its localization is also renormalizable.""",r"""In four dimensions, Yang--Mills theories with compact gauge group and power-counting renormalizable matter interactions admit perturbative renormalization compatible with gauge identities when gauge anomalies cancel. Spontaneous breaking by scalar vacuum expectation values does not by itself destroy this property. Gauging an arbitrary global symmetry without anomaly checks is not a valid universal implication.""",r"""\citet[Introduction and Sections2--5]{thooft1971massive} treats the gauge identities and explicit scalar models in1971. This is perturbative renormalizability, not construction of a nonperturbative continuum probability measure.""",('thooft1971massive',),literal='correct with missing hypotheses',verification='read the result',reconstruction='Dimension, interaction degree, anomaly cancellation and perturbative scope are supplied.')
add(563,'Vacuum manifold and symmetry breaking','P001034','definition',r"""Constant minimizers of \(U\) form \(\mathcal R\). For \(U(\phi)=\lambda(|\phi|^2-a^2)^2\), the vacuum set is a sphere; \(H_\phi\) is the subgroup fixing a vacuum.""",r"""For \(\lambda>0,a>0\) and \(\phi\in\mathbb R^n\), the minima are \(S^{n-1}_a\). When a compact group \(G\) acts transitively on a vacuum component, it is \(G/H_\phi\). This vacuum orbit is a space of Higgs field values; gauge-equivalent constant configurations need not be distinct physical vacua.""",r"""This is standard Higgs-model geometry; \citet[Section3]{thooft1971massive} explicitly analyzes the scalar minimum and residual gauge symmetry.""",('thooft1971massive',),verification='read the result',reconstruction='Positivity, transitivity, vacuum component and gauge-equivalence distinctions are made explicit.')
add(563,'Grand unification and the affine-group proposal','P001034','remark',r"""The low-energy group is \(SU(3)\times SU(2)\times U(1)\); candidates for a larger group include \(SU(5),SO(10),E_8\). Gelfand proposes an affine group, emphasizing finite rank and an invariant form.""",r"""An embedding of the indicated Lie algebra into a larger gauge algebra is only group-theoretic input. A unification model additionally requires matter representations, symmetry-breaking fields, interactions and anomaly conditions. The affine suggestion specifies none of these and therefore does not define a unique field theory or existence problem.""",r"""The finite-group mirror construction subsequently published by \citet{fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror} is an identifiable nearby model, not a realization of the unspecified affine proposal. No exact affine-model target was found in the author bibliography or prior occurrence index.""",('fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',speaker='I. M. Gelfand',status='the proposal remains underdetermined; it is not evidence of an unsolved general unification theorem.')
add(563,'Monopole sectors from the sphere at spatial infinity','P001034','construction',r"""Finite-energy fields on \(\mathbb R^3\) approach \(\mathcal R\) at infinity, defining \(S^2\to\mathcal R\), hence an element of \(\pi_2(\mathcal R)\) and monopoles.""",r"""For a Higgs model with positive potential vanishing exactly on a vacuum manifold, impose asymptotic boundary conditions ensuring that the normalized Higgs field extends continuously to the sphere at infinity. Its homotopy class lies in \(\pi_2(\mathcal R)\). Nontrivial classes distinguish sectors but do not alone prove that a finite-energy minimizing solution exists in every class.""",r"""The topology is established background to the gauge-string analysis summarized in \citet{schwarz1982charge}; the notebook compresses the analytic existence issue into the topological classification.""",('schwarz1982charge',),reconstruction='Explicit boundary regularity and the distinction between a topological sector and a solution are added.')
add(563,'Strings and fundamental-group sectors','P001034-P001035','construction',r"""A string is independent of the longitudinal coordinate and has finite energy per unit length. A linking circle maps to \(\mathcal R\); stability is followed by a question mark.""",r"""For a translationally invariant configuration on \(\mathbb R^2\times\mathbb R\), require \(\int_{\mathbb R^2}\mathcal E\,dx\,dy<\infty\) and a limiting Higgs map \(S^1_\infty\to\mathcal R\). Its class in \(\pi_1(\mathcal R)\) obstructs deformation to vacuum through admissible finite-energy configurations. This is topological stability, not a proof of spectral, nonlinear or quantum stability.""",r"""This is the vacuum-topology mechanism used in \citet{schwarz1982charge}. The scanned linking-circle diagram represents a meridian of the string, not a worldsheet boundary.""",('schwarz1982charge',),reconstruction='Boundary conditions, energy per unit length and the meaning of stability are separated.')
add(563,'Charge-reversing holonomy and the missing disconnected stabilizer','P001035','claim',r"""If an element \(\alpha\in\widetilde G\) conjugates the electromagnetic \(U(1)\) by inversion, the notes assert existence of a topologically nontrivial charge-reversing string. The unbroken group is written \(K\times U(1)\).""",r"""Let \(\widetilde G\) be simply connected and \(H\) its unbroken subgroup. A string class corresponds to a component of \(H\), since \(\pi_1(\widetilde G/H)\cong\pi_0(H)\). A representative holonomy \(\alpha\in H\) can reverse charge if \(\alpha Q\alpha^{-1}=-Q\). Merely finding \(\alpha\) in \(\widetilde G\) is insufficient: connected \(H\) gives no such fundamental-group string. Field-equation existence needs model-specific energy analysis.""",r"""The mechanism was announced in \citet{schwarz1981nonlocal}; \citet{schwarz1982charge} explicitly assumes a disconnected unbroken subgroup. The1981 announcement makes this E despite the later detailed paper.""",('schwarz1981nonlocal','schwarz1982charge'),literal='false as written',match='exact',reconstruction='The homotopy-sequence calculation and the condition alpha in H outside its identity component repair the stated criterion.',status='the Alice-string mechanism is established; the printed sufficient condition is incomplete.')
add(563,'Ordinary-to-mirror particle transport around a string','P001035','claim',r"""There are unified models containing mirror particles and strings around which a particle becomes its mirror partner. The two sectors interact weakly through heavy bosons and share gravity; mirror particles are not antiparticles.""",r"""Choose a gauge model whose disconnected residual symmetry exchanges two matter sectors. Holonomy in that component transports a state in one sector to its mirror partner. Decoupling of local nongravitational interactions at low energy is a separate statement about heavy mediator masses and couplings, not a consequence of topology alone.""",r"""\citet[pp.~427--432]{fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror} publishes the specific model in December1982, after this talk, and its abstract states precisely the sector exchange. An earlier preprint date was not verified. Classification A records the speaker's subsequent publication, with abstract-level verification.""",('fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror',),cl='A',match='exact',verification='publisher metadata checked',reconstruction='Holonomy exchange and low-energy coupling suppression are distinct assumptions.',status='the proposed gauge-theory mechanism was published; this does not establish the existence of mirror matter in nature.')
add(563,'Quantum stability of the proposed strings','P001036','question',r"""\ru{Неясно, сущ. ли квантово стабильные нити}; classical stability is asserted.""",r"""A quantum-stability question must specify the Lagrangian, coupling regime, boundary sector and whether stability means an exact conserved topological charge, absence of tunneling, a positive fluctuation spectrum or a lifetime. The notes do not select one meaning or one model.""",r"""The Schwarz and Fateev--Frolov--Schwarz publications identify nearby classical constructions; their accessible abstracts do not supply the missing quantum target. No exact quantum-stability theorem can responsibly be assigned to this sentence.""",('schwarz1982charge','fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror'),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='none found',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='medium',status='underdetermined, rather than a newly formulated universal open problem.')
add(563,'Mirror-photon visibility and the quasar anecdote','P001035-P001036','remark',r"""The diagram suggests that a string crossing a line of sight can turn photons into mirror photons. Khlopov mentions a preprint about a quasar whose luminosity changed sharply over a century.""",r"""A sector-exchanging holonomy can change which asymptotic gauge field a transported state couples to. Predicting an observed light curve requires an explicit spacetime string configuration, propagation and source model. The notebook gives no quasar name, preprint author list or measurable parameters.""",r"""The mirror-particle paper identifies the particle-transport mechanism only. The observational anecdote could not be uniquely identified from the named author and sparse description; it is not treated as experimental confirmation.""",('fateevfrolovschwarz1982mirror',),cl='F',literal='not determinable',match='thematic only',verification='unresolved lead',confidence='low',status='the particular astrophysical claim is unidentifiable from this record.')
